## Phase 5 — Backend Hardening

**Branch:** `step-6-backend-hardening`

**Objective:** Handle edge cases on the server side and close out the security gap before calling backend tracking support "done." No frontend work in this phase.

**Deliverables:**
- Fallback support: `GET /api/trips/{id}` returns enough live state (`current_lat/lng`, `current_leg_index`, status) that a client can poll it if STOMP is unavailable — this endpoint should already work post-Phase 4, just verify it
- Reasonable STOMP session handling so a dropped/reconnecting client doesn't leave orphaned state server-side
- Secrets moved out of `application.properties` into environment variables (DB password, ORS key, Geoapify key)
- Backend test suite covering: trip creation with valid/invalid stops, route precompute, one full simulated tick cycle via `pytest`/JUnit equivalent

**What to check first:**
- [ ] Confirm `GET /api/trips/{id}` already returns the tracking columns added in Phase 0 — if not, add them to `TripResponse`
- [ ] Confirm database credentials/API keys are no longer visible in the committed `application.properties`

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] No secrets remain committed to the repo
- [ ] `GET /api/trips/{id}` is a viable polling fallback on its own, independent of WebSocket
- [ ] A full simulated trip (multiple stops) runs start-to-finish via API calls alone, verified with curl/Postman, no manual intervention
- [ ] Test suite passes with reasonable coverage on the new tracking code paths

